<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grodno</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hrodna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grodno, or Hrodna, is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> river, near the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. First recorded in the 12th century, it became one of the main towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejms</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> often met; the last Sejm, in 1793, approved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a> there. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1795, to Poland between the wars and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byelorussian-ssr">Soviet Belarus</a> in 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grodno.jpg" alt="Grodno" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liashko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horadnia_(Hrodna),_Vilienskaja._%D0%93%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%BD%D1%8F,_%D0%92%D1%96%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_(2021)_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
